The mechanics, verified conceptually:

```ts
// Without generics — the type information dies
function firstAny(arr: any[]): any { return arr[0]; }   // returns any: no checking

// With generics — the relationship is CAPTURED
function first<T>(arr: T[]): T { return arr[0]; }
const n = first([7, 8, 9]);    // n: number — narrowed, checked
const s = first(['a', 'b']);   // s: string
// verified at runtime: first([7,8,9]) → 7, first(['a','b']) → 'a'

// API wrapper — the everyday production use
async function fetchJson<T>(url: string): Promise<T> {
  const res = await fetch(url);
  return res.json() as Promise<T>;   // caller declares the expected shape
}
const user = await fetchJson<User>('/api/user');   // user: User
```

The generic constraint (bounding the parameter):
```ts
function logLength<T extends { length: number }>(x: T): T {
  console.log(x.length);   // guaranteed to exist ✓
  return x;
}
logLength('hi');     // T = string (has length)
logLength([1, 2]);   // T = number[]
// logLength(42);   // ERROR: number has no length
```

When NOT to use generics (the judgment interviewers probe): if the type parameter is used only ONCE and doesn't create a relationship, it adds ceremony without safety — a plain concrete type is clearer. Generics earn their cost when the SAME declaration serves many types while keeping each use's specific type intact (containers, wrappers, mappers over typed data).
